1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,330
Let’s look at one line of code together.

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:09,160
Early Swift let you declare a variable inside the parentheses after a function name.

3
00:00:09,730 --> 00:00:13,950
If we change the number inside, what value will we see outside?

4
00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:17,590
First, we pass the number 1 into the function.

5
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:19,860
The number inside becomes 2.

6
00:00:20,430 --> 00:00:23,130
The variable outside still holds 1.

7
00:00:24,230 --> 00:00:28,740
This time we put an ampersand before the variable name in the call.

8
00:00:29,310 --> 00:00:31,010
The number inside becomes 2.

9
00:00:31,580 --> 00:00:34,700
When the function finishes, 2 is written back to the variable outside.

10
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:40,350
Ashley Garland proposed removing var from parameters and conditional bindings.

11
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:46,180
Joe Groff warned that readers could mistake the bound value for the original.

12
00:00:46,750 --> 00:00:50,740
The following year, Swift 3 removed var only from parameters.

13
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:54,600
In our opening example, the number outside stays at 1.

14
00:00:55,170 --> 00:00:59,390
We can still use a local variable. What changed is where we declare it.

15
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:01,440
We’ll stop here for today.
